    "slug": "sql-server-window-functions-advanced",
    "title": "SQL Window Functions: Running Totals, Ties and Previous Rows",
    "excerpt": "Work through a four-row sales example to distinguish row-level windows, aggregate totals and deterministic ordering.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Choose the grain before the function</h2><p>Suppose a sales ledger contains four order lines: North has order 10 for 100, order 11 for 50 and order 12 for 50; South has order 20 for 80. These are fictional amounts. You need every original line, its regional running revenue and the preceding line amount. GROUP BY would collapse the rows; a window calculation lets you retain the ledger grain. Decide whether the intended grain is an order, order line or daily summary before writing the query. A join that duplicates order lines will also inflate a window total.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Make the order deterministic</h2><p>Use a unique order key as the final ordering column. If two rows share the same timestamp, ordering only by timestamp leaves their sequence unspecified. That matters for LAG and for a row-by-row running total. The partition defines which rows are considered together; it does not remove rows from the result. An outer ORDER BY is still needed to specify the displayed order.</p><pre><code>SELECT region, order_id, amount,\n  SUM(amount) OVER (\n    PARTITION BY region ORDER BY order_id\n    ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n  ) AS running_revenue,\n  LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY order_id) AS prior_amount\nFROM dbo.Sales\nORDER BY region, order_id;</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-3\">Check the answer by hand</h2><p>For North, the expected running totals are 100, 150 and 200; prior amounts are NULL, 100 and 50. South starts a separate partition, so its running total is 80 and its prior amount is NULL. Do not replace the initial NULL with zero unless zero expresses your business definition. A first purchase has no preceding purchase; it is not necessarily a change from zero. Add a fifth North line with the same date to verify the tie-breaking behavior explicitly.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Distinguish ranking from accumulation</h2><p>ROW_NUMBER assigns different sequence numbers to tied amounts; RANK leaves a gap after ties; DENSE_RANK does not. For North amounts sorted descending, RANK yields 1, 2, 2 and DENSE_RANK also yields 1, 2, 2; add a fourth amount of 20 to expose the difference: its ranks become 4 and 3. Use an explicit ROWS frame when your question is about a sequence of individual rows. Peer-based frames answer a different question.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Investigate cost without promising a speedup</h2><p>Inspect the actual execution plan and logical reads on representative data. Ordering can require a sort; useful indexes depend on filters, partition columns and the rest of the workload. Record the input row count and result checksum before comparing timings. A faster query that changes the grain or drops ties has not solved the same problem. The SQL shown is a SQL Server example to adapt and validate in your own environment, not a benchmark result.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SQL Server OVER clause</a></li></ul>",
