    "slug": "sql-deadlock-prevention-strategies",
    "title": "SQL Server Deadlocks: Diagnose a Cycle Before Adding Retries",
    "excerpt": "Use a two-resource example to understand deadlocks, transaction ordering and safe retry boundaries.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Separate a wait from a deadlock</h2><p>A blocked transaction is waiting for a resource another transaction holds. A deadlock requires a cycle: transaction A holds the customer row and needs the invoice row, while transaction B holds the invoice row and needs the customer row. Neither can proceed by simply waiting for the other. This fictional example is about resource order, not database size. Adding a larger machine or a longer command timeout does not remove the circular dependency.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Capture the failure as evidence</h2><p>Start with the deadlock graph for the affected SQL Server workload. Read the process list, the resource list and the victim selection together. Record the statements, transaction boundaries, isolation levels and indexes involved. A screenshot of an error dialog is insufficient to establish why the cycle formed. Redact customer values before sharing a graph, because execution details can expose sensitive parameters. Reproduce the cycle only in a disposable database.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Change one cause at a time</h2><p>Make transactions that touch both resources acquire them in the same order. For example, consistently update Customer before Invoice in both application paths. Keep transactions short and avoid waiting for a remote service while holding database locks. An index may reduce the rows or pages accessed, but it is not a universal deadlock cure. Likewise, changing isolation can change consistency semantics; validate the business effect instead of applying a database-wide setting to silence one incident.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Retry the unit of work</h2><p>When a transaction is selected as a deadlock victim, its work must be reconsidered as a transaction, rather than blindly repeating the last statement. Use a bounded retry with a small randomized delay for an operation that is safe to replay. A database transaction does not automatically make an external email or payment idempotent. Place irreversible external effects behind a deliberate handoff such as an outbox and a deduplication key.</p><pre><code>attempt 1: begin transaction -&gt; update Customer -&gt; update Invoice -&gt; commit\non a confirmed deadlock victim error: roll back -&gt; wait -&gt; retry entire unit\nafter the configured attempt limit: stop and surface a diagnosable failure</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-5\">Prove the change under contention</h2><p>Run two concurrent sessions with the old resource order and capture the cycle. Then use the consistent order and confirm both business operations complete with correct balances. Repeat with an injected failure before commit. Check for missing updates, duplicate downstream events and unexpected latency. Record the test environment and sample size; absence of a deadlock in one run does not prove that every possible cycle has been eliminated.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-deadlocks-guide\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft deadlocks guide</a></li></ul>",
