    "slug": "measuring-bi-roi-financial-framework",
    "title": "Measuring BI ROI: A Worked Cost and Adoption Model",
    "excerpt": "Separate time released, cash savings and assumptions when evaluating an analytics project.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">State the counterfactual</h2><p>A fictional reporting team spends 40 hours per month producing a pack. A proposed solution is expected to reduce that to 12 hours, releasing 28 hours. Before assigning money to those hours, ask what would otherwise happen: overtime might decrease, the team might take on other work, or the hours might simply remain available. Those outcomes are economically different. A released hour is not automatically a cash saving.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">List one-time and recurring costs</h2><p>Assume an illustrative implementation cost of 6,000 currency units and recurring operating costs of 300 per month. Include internal review time, training, maintenance, licenses and capacity that the project actually requires. Do not count a shared platform invoice in full for each dashboard. Conversely, do not hide support work because it happens inside an existing team. Write down the allocation method so another person can reproduce the estimate.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Calculate a scenario rather than a testimonial</h2><p>If each released hour is valued at an assumed 25 units, gross monthly capacity value is 28 × 25 = 700. Subtracting 300 recurring cost leaves 400 in estimated monthly net capacity value. A simple implementation payback under these assumptions is 6,000 / 400 = 15 months. This is arithmetic for a hypothetical planning case, not a measured return from a client engagement. It does not account for discounting or every possible cost.</p><pre><code>Baseline monthly effort:       40 h\nExpected monthly effort:       12 h\nReleased capacity:             28 h\nAssumed value per hour:        25\nEstimated net monthly value:   28 * 25 - 300 = 400</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-4\">Test adoption sensitivity</h2><p>If the workflow is used for only half the expected reporting cycles, the assumed capacity value falls to 350 per month. With the same recurring cost, only 50 remains, extending simple payback to 120 months. That contrast is a reason to measure actual adoption rather than celebrate the theoretical efficiency of the software. Decide how usage will be observed without collecting unnecessary personal data.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Review realized outcomes</h2><p>After release, compare the same reporting pack, period length and quality standard. Track exceptions, rework and the time needed to investigate wrong totals. Keep capacity value separate from verified budget reductions and from revenue effects that have other causes. Record the confidence and owner of each assumption. The result should help a decision-maker understand uncertainty, not manufacture an impressive percentage for a project page.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-adoption-roadmap-overview\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft adoption roadmap</a></li></ul>",
