    "slug": "video-building-c-suite-dashboard",
    "title": "Executive Dashboard Walkthrough: Revenue, Margin and Exceptions",
    "excerpt": "A written design exercise for a concise executive reporting page, with explicit sample values and drill-through questions.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Define the meeting decision</h2><p>This is a written walkthrough, not a recording or a claim of a completed client delivery. Imagine a fictional weekly management meeting asking whether declining revenue requires action. Before designing visuals, agree the reporting period, currency, comparison basis and owner of the revenue definition. A dashboard cannot resolve a disagreement about whether booked orders or recognized revenue is the intended measure.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Prepare a small decision table</h2><p>Use sample values of revenue 900 against a target of 1,000, gross profit 270 and overdue receivables 120. Revenue attainment is 90%, gross margin is 30% and the target gap is 100. Label these as demonstration figures. Do not call overdue receivables bad debt: overdue describes timing, while collectability requires more evidence. Keep the source table accessible so a reviewer can reproduce each number.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Arrange the page by questions</h2><p>Put the period and freshness indicator near the headline metrics. Follow them with a revenue trend and a breakdown that can explain the target gap. End with an exception list containing a responsible owner and a next action. A large decorative chart is less useful than a small table showing which customers or products account for the change. Avoid making every indicator red when only one decision needs attention.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Separate reports from service dashboards</h2><p>In Power BI terminology, an interactive report page and a Power BI service dashboard are different artifacts. If viewers need slicers, detailed filters and multiple pages, design that report experience rather than promising those behaviors on every dashboard tile. Decide where a selection should lead and test the destination with a viewer account. Include text labels for status; color alone is not enough for every reader.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Run a five-minute acceptance session</h2><p>Ask a reviewer to identify the target gap, explain the margin denominator and find the owner of the largest overdue item. Do not coach them during the task. Note ambiguous labels, excessive clicks and any case where a stale period was mistaken for current data. Test an empty period and a narrow screen. Successful acceptance means the intended decisions are supported; it does not establish a revenue improvement caused by the design.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/service-dashboards\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Power BI dashboard and report distinctions</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WCAG 2.2</a></li></ul>",
