    "slug": "cdo-playbook-2026-strategy",
    "title": "A Practical CDO Operating Plan: Owners, Decisions and Exceptions",
    "excerpt": "Translate a data strategy into accountable work using one shared metric and an explicit escalation path.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Choose one disagreement to resolve</h2><p>A fictional retailer has three different definitions of an active customer. Sales counts anyone with a purchase in twelve months, marketing counts recent email engagement and finance counts an open account balance. A strategy document cannot make those definitions interchangeable. Begin by identifying which decision each definition supports. Preserve legitimate differences and give them distinct names instead of forcing one ambiguous number into every report.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Create a decision record</h2><p>For the sales definition, record the time window, source system, treatment of returns and accountable business owner. Name a technical steward who maintains the calculation, but do not transfer the business definition to that steward by default. Include an example customer who changes classification at a period boundary. Ask stakeholders to approve the example results, not merely the wording in a glossary.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Fund operating work as well as delivery</h2><p>A metric needs monitoring, access reviews, source-change handling and someone to answer discrepancies after launch. Estimate that work alongside initial development. Maintain a small queue with severity and decision deadlines. An expired access request and a disagreement about revenue recognition should not disappear into the same undifferentiated ticket list. Set an escalation path for issues that the steward cannot decide alone.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Measure an observable improvement</h2><p>Use measures such as time to resolve a definition dispute, the number of overdue ownership decisions and the proportion of critical pipelines with a named responder. Avoid treating the size of a catalog as evidence of trust. For an illustrative month, resolving eight of ten assigned disputes is 80% closure, but it says nothing about whether the two remaining disputes are the most consequential ones. Inspect severity and recurrence alongside the rate.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Review the operating model</h2><p>At a monthly review, choose one incident and trace how it moved from detection to resolution. Did the owner have authority? Did downstream consumers learn about the correction? Did a source change bypass the agreed process? Update the decision record and remove redundant governance steps where evidence shows they add delay without improving decisions. The deliverable is a working accountability loop, not a prediction about how every enterprise will organize in 2030.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-adoption-roadmap-overview\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft adoption roadmap</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "da3d85b057622d16353828c58644f7c1928ccbe9bcd96eeeefb547896e5548aa"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "The 2026 CDO Playbook: Leading with a Data-First Mindset"
    ],
