    "slug": "scaling-data-culture-enterprise",
    "title": "Building Data Champions: A Four-Week Evidence-Based Pilot",
    "excerpt": "Design a small learning program around real reporting tasks and observable support outcomes.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Recruit around tasks</h2><p>Imagine a fictional operations team that repeatedly asks analysts to reconcile weekly shipment counts. Recruit two willing colleagues who already help others with that task. A champion is not automatically the person with the highest job title or the most certificates. Give the participants protected time, a named analyst contact and permission to escalate uncertainty rather than expecting them to answer every data question.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Plan four small sessions</h2><p>In week one, reproduce the shipment count from a known snapshot. In week two, investigate a deliberately duplicated shipment key. In week three, explain why two reports disagree about cancelled orders. In week four, teach the same diagnostic process to a colleague. Keep the exercises tied to the team's data definitions. A broad presentation about becoming “data driven” would not reveal whether participants can solve the actual reconciliation problem.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Observe without ranking people</h2><p>Record whether the participant selected the right period, checked the grain and found the agreed cancellation rule. Use fictional or sanitized records, and collect only the learning information needed to improve the program. Avoid publishing individual league tables that punish people for asking questions. A reasonable outcome is that a participant recognizes a boundary and escalates it correctly, rather than guessing an answer to look confident.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Measure support demand carefully</h2><p>Suppose the sample exercise takes 30 minutes before training and 18 minutes afterward. The twelve-minute difference is an observation for that exercise, not a guaranteed saving on every future ticket. Watch for analysts spending extra time correcting confidently wrong answers. Count repeat requests and reopened issues as well as initial completions. Separate attendance, self-reported confidence and demonstrated task success in the review.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Build a maintainable knowledge loop</h2><p>Let champions propose short notes with the question, example, answer and owner. Have a qualified reviewer check the note before it becomes shared guidance. Date the underlying definition and retire superseded instructions. Rotate responsibilities so one enthusiastic employee does not become an unpaid support desk. Expand the program only when the pilot provides evidence of useful support and participants have the capacity to continue.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-adoption-roadmap-overview\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft adoption roadmap</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "c6fd15a147b54d37311de2a63f236e3cfdea260e0f515f00276817ec2b9af172"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "Scaling Data Culture: From Power Users to Data Champions"
    ],
