    "slug": "video-fabric-end-to-end",
    "title": "Fabric Implementation Walkthrough: From a CSV Batch to a Checked Report",
    "excerpt": "A written end-to-end practice plan with control totals, model checks and a recovery exercise.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Set a small acceptance target</h2><p>This is a written walkthrough, not an embedded video or a record of a client deployment. Prepare a fictional CSV with order key, order date, product, quantity and unit price. Use three lines with amounts 100, 150 and 50, for a control total of 300. Write that result in a separate test note before importing the data. The acceptance target is a report that reproduces the total and explains any rejected row.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Land and validate the batch</h2><p>Choose a Fabric data store appropriate for the authorized environment and your maintenance approach. Retain a raw copy and a batch identifier, then convert dates and numbers explicitly in a preparation step. Add a duplicate key and a malformed date to a separate test batch. Decide whether either condition blocks publication. A pipeline status of succeeded describes execution; it does not establish that the resulting business totals are correct.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Build a model you can explain</h2><p>Separate dimensions from facts when that makes the relationships clearer. Identify the fact-table grain and verify dimension keys are unique. Define a revenue measure from the prepared amount or the agreed quantity-times-price expression. Check the unfiltered total of 300, then filter each product and reconcile the displayed lines. Do not add multiple filter paths merely to make a single demonstration visual appear responsive.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Share as a viewer</h2><p>Test the report using an authorized viewer account with the intended permissions. Verify that data access matches the requirement and that a restricted account cannot reach an underlying asset by following a different URL. Document the model storage mode actually used; do not infer Direct Lake from a workspace name. Avoid publishing the practice report with real customer data through a publicly accessible embed.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Practice an interruption</h2><p>Fail the next source batch deliberately, then confirm the report's freshness label does not pretend that old data is current. Restore the source and replay with the same batch key. Check that the replay does not double the total. Save the transformation notes, model sketch, expected totals and recovery steps together. Those artifacts make the exercise repeatable without claiming that one short tutorial covers every production requirement.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/microsoft-fabric-overview\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft Fabric overview</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Power BI star schema guidance</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "7ef53f8eb72e64661ec2fda7cdaa723b08e8950c3a20786f6be2c21deb2c7fcf"
    ],
