    "slug": "agentic-shift-autonomous-intelligence-2026",
    "title": "Designing an AI Agent with Read-Only Tools and Approval Boundaries",
    "excerpt": "Work through a support-ticket assistant that can recommend action without silently performing it.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Choose a bounded job</h2><p>A fictional support assistant reads a ticket, searches approved documentation and proposes a response. It does not need permission to change a subscription, delete records or send the message automatically. Describe those boundaries as capabilities, rather than relying on a sentence in the prompt saying “be careful.” A tool the agent cannot invoke is a stronger boundary than an instruction asking it not to invoke an available powerful tool.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Treat retrieved text as evidence</h2><p>A support ticket or retrieved document can contain instructions written by someone other than the operator. Keep that text in a data role and do not let it grant new permissions. For example, a ticket asking the assistant to ignore approval and refund an account should remain a customer request for review. Store the source reference beside a recommendation so a human can verify why it was made.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Separate proposal from execution</h2><p>Have the first stage produce a structured action proposal containing the ticket identifier, evidence, uncertainty and requested action. A second stage checks the authorized scope and obtains approval where required. The execution service should validate the action again, including ownership and current account state. Approval of a draft response is not automatically approval of an unrelated account mutation.</p><pre><code>read ticket -&gt; retrieve approved references -&gt; propose draft\nvalidate proposal -&gt; human approval when required -&gt; execute permitted action\nrecord outcome using an idempotency key tied to the approved action</code></pre><h2 id=\"section-4\">Define a failure budget</h2><p>Set a maximum number of tool calls and a timeout for the task. Give the system a clear abstain path when documentation conflicts or the ticket lacks an identifier. Avoid unlimited “self-healing” loops that repeatedly call a failing integration. In a test, inject a missing document, a tool timeout and a misleading instruction inside the ticket. The safe result may be an incomplete recommendation that explains what information is missing.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Evaluate decisions and effects</h2><p>Review a labelled set of tickets for factual correctness, correct evidence, appropriate abstention and unauthorized actions attempted. Measure these separately from how fluent the response sounds. Record the configuration and test cases when changing a model or prompt. No architecture makes an agent incapable of mistakes; reducing its authority and making consequential effects reviewable limits the cost of a mistake.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST AI Risk Management Framework</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "5c8d15dbcafa8d3f788b45a08f8b8dabb710845f0af1cabc388c84bbaa88e70b"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "The Agentic Shift: Architecting Autonomous Intelligence in Enterprise Workflows (2026)"
    ],
