    "slug": "real-time-streaming-analytics-power-bi",
    "title": "Streaming Analytics: Measure Freshness, Handle Late Events and Keep a Batch Check",
    "excerpt": "A worked event-timeline exercise for designing a dashboard whose freshness claim can be verified.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Name the clocks</h2><p>A fictional parcel scanner records an event at 10:00:00, the broker receives it at 10:00:02 and the reporting store commits it at 10:00:05. A viewer sees the refreshed visual at 10:00:12. Those timestamps describe different delays. Calling the system “sub-second” because one internal stage took less than a second would misrepresent the end-to-end experience.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Choose the business timestamp</h2><p>Decide whether a five-minute chart groups parcels by scan time or by arrival time. Event time may better represent operations, but late-arriving events can change an earlier bucket. Arrival time is simpler for some monitoring tasks but answers when the platform learned of the event. Keep both timestamps when appropriate, and document the treatment of clock skew and missing event times.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Deduplicate and handle revisions</h2><p>Assign each event a stable identifier and test a redelivery. Two deliveries of the same scan should not automatically become two parcels. Define whether a later correction replaces a previous event, adds a compensating event or is rejected for review. A transport guarantee does not by itself prove that a business count is correct. Include a durable reconciliation path back to the source records.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Check an illustrative timeline</h2><p>If three unique scan identifiers arrive, followed by a duplicate of the second, the unique-event count remains three. If one original scan belongs to the previous five-minute bucket, decide when that bucket may be updated and how the viewer learns that the data is provisional. Use these small cases before attempting a high-volume load test. The expected counts are test-fixture results, not production throughput measurements.</p><h2 id=\"section-5\">Display freshness and failure honestly</h2><p>Expose the latest successfully processed source timestamp and an indicator for pipeline failure or backlog. Choose a visual refresh mechanism supported by the actual product configuration. Microsoft Fabric Real-Time Intelligence offers event-oriented capabilities, but product selection does not guarantee a particular dashboard latency. Compare the streaming result with a periodic batch reconciliation and investigate differences before advertising an end-to-end service level.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/overview\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft Fabric Real-Time Intelligence</a></li></ul>",
    "sourceHashes": [
      "6115cb2b3361ae52f9d44234f75cfcd44c561454d84cd7a51edde3913a74e08d"
    ],
    "sourceTitles": [
      "Real-Time Streaming Analytics: Sub-Second Dashboards"
    ],
