    "slug": "cybersecurity-bi-data-vault-hardening",
    "title": "Secure BI Access: Restrict Queries, Exports and Service Credentials",
    "excerpt": "A practical reporting-service review with negative tests and a minimal audit trail.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Identify every way to reach the data</h2><p>A fictional BI application offers report viewing, an export button and a query API. Restricting the visible report alone does not protect data if the export or API accepts a broader request. List each access path and the identity used at that boundary. Do not treat an iframe, a hidden route or a regional filter as proof that the underlying dataset is isolated.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Reduce runtime authority</h2><p>Use a service identity with only the operations required for the reporting workload. Keep administrative and migration credentials separate from normal read access. Store secrets in the deployment environment and avoid embedding them in client bundles, URLs or logs. If a connection pool is used, confirm how session-specific security state is reset before that connection is reused by another request.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Test the denial paths</h2><p>Use authorized test identities for a permitted region, an unauthorized region and a revoked account. Try the report, export and direct API with each. Verify that caches, error messages and downloaded files preserve the same boundary. A failed request should return an appropriate denial without revealing restricted totals or sensitive SQL parameters.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Keep evidence proportional</h2><p>Record request identifiers, authorization outcomes and the configuration version needed to diagnose an incident. Avoid logging entire customer rows unnecessarily. Test the controls again after deployment or a role-model change. This guide does not claim a 55% response-time reduction, a particular concurrency capacity or a measured CPU ceiling; performance testing and security validation require their own documented workloads and results.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/zero-trust-architecture\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Zero Trust Architecture</a></li></ul>",
