    "slug": "sales-performance-ecosystem-2026",
    "title": "Sales Reporting Design: Targets, Returns and Reconciliation",
    "excerpt": "Construct a small sales reporting model whose definitions can be checked against source records.",
    "content": "<aside>Worked example with fictional data. AI-assisted editorial revision; no client performance results are claimed.</aside><h2 id=\"section-1\">Agree the unit of analysis</h2><p>A fictional sales report tracks order lines, not just customers or invoices. Define whether revenue is booked, shipped or recognized, and specify how returns are represented. A target comparison is meaningful only when actuals and targets use compatible periods, currencies and scope. This guide describes a design exercise rather than a verified client engagement or a measured reporting-time saving.</p><h2 id=\"section-2\">Use a transparent example</h2><p>Assume three net sales amounts of 100, 80 and -20 for a return. Net sales are 160. Against an illustrative target of 200, attainment is 80% and the gap is 40. Gross positive order value is 180, which answers a different question. Put the definitions next to the metrics so the reader does not confuse an attainment percentage with an efficiency improvement.</p><h2 id=\"section-3\">Keep dimensions from multiplying facts</h2><p>Use stable keys for product, salesperson and reporting date. Check uniqueness on the dimension side before joining. If a salesperson has multiple historical assignments, decide which assignment applies to the order date instead of joining every assignment to every order. Reconcile line counts and net revenue after each transformation.</p><h2 id=\"section-4\">Design the review path</h2><p>Allow a user to inspect the transactions behind a changed total, with access appropriate to their role. Show the source cutoff and unresolved reconciliation exceptions. Test an empty period, a return larger than the original sale and a changed target. Evaluate preparation and review effort with real observations before publishing a business-impact claim.</p><h2 id=\"references\">Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Power BI star schema guidance</a></li></ul>",
